nir/opt_varyings: propagate a constant into a fragment shader only when the whole slot goes away

Blob evidence. The Demo's star shader (program 13) has `v_uv = vec2(0.5, a_corner.y * 0.5 + 0.5)` in
its vertex shader and samples `texture2D(u_tex, v_uv)` in its fragment shader. The blob keeps the
whole vec2 in the varying and samples straight out of the loaded slot, two instructions in total
(capture/demo/compare-26.2.6/prog-13.blob.pp.txt):
    load.v $0.xyz 0
    load.v ^discard 1.xy, texld 0, mov.s1 $0.w ^const0.y,
    mul.s2 $0.xyz ^texture.w $0.xyzz, const0 0 1 0 0, sync, stop
Its varying descriptor for that program is stride 16 over 2 vec4 slots, i.e. it does write the
constant component out of the vertex shader rather than fold it into the fragment shader.

What lima did. nir_opt_varyings' propagate_uniform_expressions() moved the 0.5 into the fragment
shader and dropped that component of the slot. The slot is still loaded for v_uv.y, so the texture
coordinate can no longer be the loaded varying: lima had to materialize 0.5 and move both components
into a register first, two extra instructions on every fragment:
    mov.s1 $1.x ^const0.x, const0 0.5 ...
    load.v $0.xy 0.xy, mov.s1 $1.y $0.y
    load.v $2.xyz 1
    load.v ^discard. $1.xyxx, texld 0, mul.v0 ..., mov.s1 $0.w ^const0.x, sync, stop

This patch adds the io_option nir_io_dont_propagate_partial_fs_inputs: with it set on the fragment
shader, a scalar slot is propagated only when every scalar slot of its vec4 slot that the fragment
shader still loads would be propagated too, so the optimization keeps its win (the whole slot
disappears) and loses the pessimization (the slot stays and a component has to be rebuilt). The
candidate test in the pre-pass is a superset of the main loop's, so no component that would have been
propagated before is skipped now. lima's fragment options set the flag.

Effect. Demo program 13: 4 -> 2 PP instructions, the blob's count; Demo total 46 -> 44 (the blob is
45), 278 -> 270 code words. Its vertex shader goes 66 -> 64 GP instructions, so the Demo's GP total
falls 589 -> 587 as well. Fill-weighted against the blob's counters: ignition 7.06 -> 6.53 ms per
frame (x1.14 -> x1.00 of the blob; program 13 covers the stars, 25 200 vertices a frame), and the
other scenes move only through programs 4 and 43, already counted under 0010. reference UI counts unchanged.
Note the vertex shader now writes the constant component again: +0 varying slots, so no extra varying
bytes per vertex.

Pictures: expected identical, with one dependency worth naming. The fragment shader reads an
interpolated varying whose value is the same at all three vertices instead of a literal 0.5. The PP's
interpolator evaluates a*dx + b*dy + c with a = b = 0 for a constant varying, so it returns the
stored 0.5 exactly; the blob's own picture depends on the same property. If the interpolator were to
perturb a constant varying, the texture coordinate would move by that amount, so the frozen-shot
compare below is the check.

Applies to Mesa 26.2.6 + 0001-0011.
diff --git a/src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_varyings.c b/src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_varyings.c
index 542add2..dc9c0bb 100644
--- a/src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_varyings.c
+++ b/src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_varyings.c
@@ -2890,11 +2890,61 @@ propagate_uniform_expressions(struct linkage_info *linkage,
                               bool *consumer_progress)
 {
    unsigned i;
+   BITSET_DECLARE(whole_slot_only, NUM_SCALAR_SLOTS);
+
+   /* nir_io_dont_propagate_partial_fs_inputs: find the scalar slots that could
+    * be propagated, so the loop below can tell whether propagating one of them
+    * removes the whole vec4 slot from the fragment shader or only part of it.
+    * The test here is deliberately a superset of the loop's (it leaves out the
+    * color and texcoord restrictions), so a slot is never skipped for a
+    * component that would have been propagated anyway.
+    */
+   BITSET_ZERO(whole_slot_only);
+   if (linkage->consumer_stage == MESA_SHADER_FRAGMENT &&
+       linkage->consumer_builder.shader->options->io_options &
+       nir_io_dont_propagate_partial_fs_inputs) {
+      BITSET_DECLARE(candidate, NUM_SCALAR_SLOTS);
+      BITSET_ZERO(candidate);
+
+      BITSET_FOREACH_SET(i, linkage->output_equal_mask, NUM_SCALAR_SLOTS) {
+         if (!can_optimize_varying(linkage, vec4_slot(i)).propagate_uniform_expr)
+            continue;
+
+         struct is_uniform_expr_state state = {
+            .linkage = linkage,
+            .cost = 0,
+         };
+
+         nir_shader_clear_pass_flags(linkage->producer_builder.shader);
+         if (is_uniform_expression(nir_def_instr(linkage->slot[i].producer.value.def),
+                                   &state) &&
+             state.cost <= linkage->max_varying_expression_cost)
+            BITSET_SET(candidate, i);
+      }
+
+      /* A candidate is only propagated if every scalar slot of its vec4 slot
+       * that the fragment shader still loads is a candidate too.
+       */
+      BITSET_FOREACH_SET(i, candidate, NUM_SCALAR_SLOTS) {
+         unsigned base = i & ~0x7u;
+
+         for (unsigned j = base; j < base + 8; j++) {
+            if (j != i && !BITSET_TEST(candidate, j) &&
+                !list_is_empty(&linkage->slot[j].consumer.loads)) {
+               BITSET_SET(whole_slot_only, i);
+               break;
+            }
+         }
+      }
+   }
 
    /* Find uniform expressions. If there are multiple stores, they should all
     * store the same value. That's guaranteed by output_equal_mask.
     */
    BITSET_FOREACH_SET(i, linkage->output_equal_mask, NUM_SCALAR_SLOTS) {
+      if (BITSET_TEST(whole_slot_only, i))
+         continue;
+
       if (!can_optimize_varying(linkage, vec4_slot(i)).propagate_uniform_expr)
          continue;
 
diff --git a/src/compiler/nir/nir_shader_compiler_options.h b/src/compiler/nir/nir_shader_compiler_options.h
index 9de8a4f..8066100 100644
--- a/src/compiler/nir/nir_shader_compiler_options.h
+++ b/src/compiler/nir/nir_shader_compiler_options.h
@@ -241,6 +241,20 @@ typedef enum {
     */
    nir_io_dont_compact_fs_inputs = BITFIELD_BIT(14),
 
+   /**
+    * Set in a fragment shader's options: nir_opt_varyings propagates
+    * a constant or uniform expression into the fragment shader only when that
+    * removes the whole vec4 slot. Propagating one component of a slot the
+    * fragment shader still loads is a pessimization on hardware that loads a
+    * varying one vec4 slot at a time and can feed a texture coordinate
+    * straight out of the loaded slot (Mali-400 PP): the slot is loaded
+    * anyway, and the propagated component now has to be materialized in
+    * a register and moved next to the components that stayed, which costs
+    * instructions on every fragment. The Mali blob keeps such a component in
+    * the varying.
+    */
+   nir_io_dont_propagate_partial_fs_inputs = BITFIELD_BIT(15),
+
    /* Options affecting the GLSL compiler or Gallium are below. */
 
    /**
diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_program.c b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_program.c
index 42907c0..7639fa0 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_program.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_program.c
@@ -87,7 +87,8 @@ static const nir_shader_compiler_options fs_nir_options = {
    .max_varying_expression_cost = 2,
    /* the PP loads a varying one vec4 slot at a time: keep the linker's
     * aligned layout (prefer_pot_aligned_varyings), as the blob does */
-   .io_options = nir_io_dont_compact_fs_inputs,
+   .io_options = nir_io_dont_compact_fs_inputs |
+                 nir_io_dont_propagate_partial_fs_inputs,
 };
 
 const void *
